issues.quote:
**Disposition: defect in the local pagination predicate.** The contract says a cursor denotes the last emitted row, so the next page must select rows strictly greater than the cursor. In `query.mjs`, `x >= cursor` includes that row again.

A reproducible witness, expressed as an executable assertion:

```js
import assert from 'node:assert/strict';
import { page } from './page.mjs';

const rows = [1, 2, 3];
const first = page(rows, undefined, 2);
const second = page(rows, first.cursor, 2);

assert.deepEqual(first.items, [1, 2]);
assert.deepEqual(second.items, [3]); // Predicted actual: [2, 3]
```

The source predicts that the second page repeats `2`, violating the exactly-once obligation. With page size `1`, the same predicate also returns the cursor row repeatedly, so pagination cannot advance. Changing `x >= cursor` to `x > cursor` addresses this predicate defect.

These are static predictions from the supplied source; the assertion was **not run**. The local evidence does not establish a deployed impact.

